<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The player's on-screen controls, as a sibling of the navigation shell rather than a page in its
  frame: the pane and the header must not stay on screen behind a film.

  Everything here is drawn over a transparent island with mpv's child HWND showing through it. That
  is what fact 1 in HostWindow's remarks bought, and it is why this file replaces the WinForms
  shell's five hand-painted layered top-level windows (PlayerBar 1291 lines, PlayerFlyout 813,
  VolumeRail 283, SkipSectionButton 205, Chrome 696) with declarative XAML in one visual tree. The
  four consequences worth naming:

    * Requirement 1, resizing. Nine SetWindowPos calls per WM_SIZE became one MoveAndResize for the
      island plus one for the video child. The chrome resizes because it is laid out, not moved.
    * Requirement 12, the mess when switching episodes. The island is above the video by
      construction, so nothing has to be brought to the front and the handover cover cannot lose a
      z-order race it used to lose.
    * Requirements 9/10/11, the reveal rule. Real pointer events arrive here, so ChromeReveal is
      driven one event at a time instead of off a 40 ms Cursor.Position poll.
    * The pickers. MenuFlyout gets a genuine DesktopAcrylicBackdrop from the framework's own
      generic.xaml, keyboard navigation and narrator names, none of which PlayerFlyout had.

  No transitions and no fades on the two overlays the pointer's own bands reveal: requirement 9 was
  「不要淡入淡出了，鼠标移动到对应位置直接显示」, so the transport bar and the title strip are Visibility
  flips. The volume rail is the exception the user asked for afterwards —
  「加大音量条的尺寸，显示方式改为淡入淡出，鼠标指针越接近右边的中心显示越明显」 — and it fades through an
  implicit ScalarTransition on its own Opacity, explained where it is declared. The one storyboard in
  the file is PulseBadge's, which nobody reveals — it is the one-second 暂停/播放 acknowledgement the
  user asked to have an animation, and it explains itself down there.

  Every readout here is bound to PlayerViewModel with x:Bind rather than assigned from code-behind —
  the clock, the seek and volume sliders, the play glyph, the source line, the skip offer, the chapter
  peek and the handover cover — and the transport buttons are Command bindings. What is left as a
  Click= or an Opened= handler is there because it is a WinUI behaviour rather than player state: the
  window commands, the fullscreen and 置顶 toggles, the six menus (whose rows are built on open), the
  seek track's own size and hover, and the mute button, which flashes the volume rail as well as
  muting. Visibility on Bar/TitleStrip/ThinLine and the rail's Opacity stay code-owned for the same
  reason: they are the reveal rule's output, not a property of what is playing.

  The scrims are gradients rather than an in-app AcrylicBrush, and deliberately. WinUI 3 has no
  host-backdrop acrylic, so an AcrylicBrush here would sample the transparent island above the video
  rather than the picture, and blur nothing — it would come out a flat tint pretending to be a
  material. A gradient is also simply the better answer over arbitrary video: acrylic's luminosity
  tint washes out over a bright frame, and a scrim that fades to nothing keeps the picture readable
  right up to the controls.
-->
<UserControl
    x:Class="EmbyNian.Shell.Views.PlayerPage"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:local="using:EmbyNian.Shell.Views"
    xmlns:vm="using:EmbyNian.Shell.ViewModels"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    mc:Ignorable="d"
    RequestedTheme="Dark"
    FontFamily="{StaticResource EgUiFontFamily}"
    FontSize="{StaticResource EgBodyFontSize}"
    IsTabStop="True"
    UseSystemFocusVisuals="False"
    Visibility="Collapsed">

    <UserControl.Resources>
        <!-- Turns the slider's own 0..1000 into a clock, which is what makes hovering the seek bar
             say 「1:07:32」 for free. A stateful converter because the duration is the file's, not the
             slider's; the page keeps it current. -->
        <local:SeekClockConverter x:Key="SeekClockConverter" />

        <!-- ================= The palette =================
             Eighteen brushes and two scrims, every one of them declared without a colour: the values
             are PlayerPalette's, in Core, and PaintPalette fills these shells in from that table right
             after InitializeComponent. A SolidColorBrush is a shared object and Color is a dependency
             property, so painting it there reaches every StaticResource consumer below — the same
             mechanism DetailPage.PaintScrim uses for the hero's gradient.

             Why Core and not here: thirty-five literals lived in this file, nine distinct colours
             written between two and seven times each, and nothing could check them. The table can be
             unit-tested — the ink rungs really do descend in luminance, the three panels really do
             ascend in opacity, the ticks really are brighter than the buffered fill — and the test
             project cannot reference this assembly, only Core.

             A brush per role rather than a colour per use also matters for the ticks: the chapter marks
             are rebuilt on every resize and every chapter list, and a hundred brushes for a hundred
             chapters is a hundred more composition objects than the picture needs.

             Unpainted, a SolidColorBrush is transparent — invisible text, not a crash — which is why
             ProbePalette walks this dictionary against the same table on every self-check. -->
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerInkBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerInkSoftBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerInkDimBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerInkFaintBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerStatsBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerRailBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerPeekBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerSkipBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerCoverBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerEdgeFaintBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerEdgeBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerEdgeStrongBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerTickBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerTrackBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerTrackFillBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerLineBrush" />
        <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PlayerPulseBrush" />

        <!-- Top to bottom in both cases, and the stops come from the table too: the transport's scrim
             deepens downward and the title strip's fades out, so a direction reversed by an edit here
             would be a hard band across the middle of the picture rather than an obvious mistake. -->
        <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="PlayerBottomScrim" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1" />
        <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="PlayerTopScrim" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1" />

        <!-- Flat, borderless and see-through until pointed at: over a picture, a button's own filled
             background is more visible than its glyph and reads as a panel rather than a control.

             The focus rectangle is asked for explicitly here. The page itself refuses one (see
             UseSystemFocusVisuals on the UserControl above) because the page is a tab stop — it has to be,
             the single-letter commands are its own — and a ring drawn around the whole film would be
             absurd. That reasoning does not extend to the controls inside it: someone driving the transport
             from the keyboard has no pointer to look at, so the only thing telling them which button Space
             will press is this rectangle. -->
        <Style x:Key="OsdButtonStyle" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
            <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource PlayerInkBrush}" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,6" />
            <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="40" />
            <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
            <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource EgUiFontFamily}" />
            <Setter Property="UseSystemFocusVisuals" Value="True" />
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="OsdGlyphStyle" TargetType="FontIcon">
            <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="Segoe Fluent Icons,Segoe MDL2 Assets" />
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="16" />
        </Style>

        <!-- ================= 暂停/播放 那两个形状 =================
             「暂停和开始的图标太丑了弄一个白色三角形方块和两个白色的长方块就可以」, and 「太丑」 was measurable
             rather than a matter of taste: U+E769 and U+E768 in Segoe Fluent Icons are *outline* glyphs — the
             pause one carries four contours, two outer boxes with two inner boxes cut out of them, wall
             thickness 128 of 2048 units, which at the 140 the badge used comes to 8.75 pixels. Blown up to a
             third of the picture's height that is a wireframe drawing, not an icon. The fallback font is the
             same story. What the request asks for is the solid shapes underneath.

             The geometry is not here. It is PulseArt's, in Core, because 「what weight class does this sit in」
             is arithmetic and the answer has to stay in the one the user recognized two shapes ago — each
             figure keeps its corner polygon, so Bounds reads the box off that (the rounded outline is cut
             inward and never exceeds it), and PlaybackTests pins it, centred.

             The outlines carry their rounded corners in the geometry itself (tangent cut plus a tangent arc at
             every corner), which the previous generation could not do: it drew a sharp polygon and let a
             round-join stroke of fixed width fatten it, which caps every corner at half the stroke width — six
             pixels of rounding on a 136 box, a wireframe's idea of soft. The arc trick is also what the five
             title-bar icons in ShellPage.xaml could not have used — a PathIcon has no arcs — which is why those
             needed an awk script to work out their corners. -->

        <!-- The 暂停/播放 badge's fifth of a second, and the only storyboard in the file — see PulseBadge for
             why this one is not the fade the reveal rule refuses.

             It was a whole second: 「暂停后显示一秒暂停图标就行（开启播放也弄个一秒的动画）」 was the request
             that put it there, and 「把暂和开始的动画改为 0.2 秒」 is the one that shortened it. Both are kept
             above because the second only makes sense as an edit to the first.

             The three phases are re-cut rather than scaled by a fifth. Scaling would give 18 ms to arrive —
             one frame at 60 Hz, which reads as a hard cut, not as an animation — and 76 ms to leave. So:
             50 ms up, 60 ms at full, 90 ms out. The fade takes nearly half of the fifth of a second because
             it is the whole of 「it left by itself」; under about 80 ms it stops reading as a fade at all.

             The scale travels less far so that it travels at the same speed: 0.72 → 1 over 220 ms was 0.00127
             per millisecond, and 0.86 → 1 over 110 ms is the same figure, so the growth feels exactly as it
             did. The drift outward cannot keep that speed (0.18 over 780 ms would be 0.02 over 90 ms, which is
             nothing), so 1.06 is a compromise — still drifting outward rather than retreating, which is the
             thing that makes it read as an acknowledgement rather than as a cancellation.

             Opacity and scale are both composition properties, so this runs off the UI thread and needs no
             EnableDependentAnimation. Completed collapses the badge: left visible at zero opacity it would
             still be a hit-test-free but laid-out element on every subsequent frame. -->
        <Storyboard x:Key="PulseStoryboard" Completed="OnPulseCompleted">
            <DoubleAnimationUsingKeyFrames
                Storyboard.TargetName="PulseBadge"
                Storyboard.TargetProperty="Opacity">
                <LinearDoubleKeyFrame KeyTime="0:0:0" Value="0" />
                <LinearDoubleKeyFrame KeyTime="0:0:0.05" Value="1" />
                <LinearDoubleKeyFrame KeyTime="0:0:0.11" Value="1" />
                <LinearDoubleKeyFrame KeyTime="0:0:0.2" Value="0" />
            </DoubleAnimationUsingKeyFrames>
            <DoubleAnimationUsingKeyFrames
                Storyboard.TargetName="PulseScale"
                Storyboard.TargetProperty="ScaleX">
                <LinearDoubleKeyFrame KeyTime="0:0:0" Value="0.86" />
                <SplineDoubleKeyFrame KeyTime="0:0:0.11" KeySpline="0.1,0.9 0.2,1" Value="1" />
                <LinearDoubleKeyFrame KeyTime="0:0:0.2" Value="1.06" />
            </DoubleAnimationUsingKeyFrames>
            <DoubleAnimationUsingKeyFrames
                Storyboard.TargetName="PulseScale"
                Storyboard.TargetProperty="ScaleY">
                <LinearDoubleKeyFrame KeyTime="0:0:0" Value="0.86" />
                <SplineDoubleKeyFrame KeyTime="0:0:0.11" KeySpline="0.1,0.9 0.2,1" Value="1" />
                <LinearDoubleKeyFrame KeyTime="0:0:0.2" Value="1.06" />
            </DoubleAnimationUsingKeyFrames>
        </Storyboard>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!-- Transparent, not null: a null background is not hit-testable, and every pointer event the
         reveal rule runs on lands on this grid. It is also what lets the video show through.

         A PictureSurface rather than a Grid, and it is a Grid with one thing added — a way in to
         UIElement.ProtectedCursor, which is protected and therefore only reachable from a derived type. That
         is what actually hides the mouse pointer over the picture: while the pointer is over XAML content the
         shape on screen is the framework's, and nothing this process does on the UI thread is on that path.
         See PictureSurface and HostWindow.BlankInputCursor. -->
    <local:PictureSurface x:Name="Root" Background="Transparent" SizeChanged="OnRootResized">

        <!-- ================= 右键画面菜单 =================
             The tree is PlayerMenuCatalog's, harvested from the bundled input.conf's own uosc menu, so
             「右键 → 画面 → 开/关 裁切填充」 is where someone who has used that configuration expects it.
             Built on first open rather than in XAML because it is a hundred-odd rows of data, and as a
             ContextFlyout rather than a RightTapped handler so the menu key and Shift+F10 reach it too. -->
        <local:PictureSurface.ContextFlyout>
            <MenuFlyout x:Name="PictureMenu" Opening="OnPictureMenuOpening" />
        </local:PictureSurface.ContextFlyout>

        <!-- ================= 顶部：标题与窗口操作 =================
             The three window commands are the last column rather than a separate overlay of the shell's,
             and that is the whole of 「全屏后右上角的图标不会自动隐藏，鼠标移到左上角时会跟置顶跟统计重叠」:
             an overlay outside this strip could neither come and go with the reveal rule nor be kept out
             of 统计/置顶's corner, and it did both wrong at once. As a column it hides with the strip and
             the grid guarantees no two of them can ever share a pixel.

             The strip pads only its left edge, so those buttons reach the top-right corner the way every
             other window's do; everything else in the row carries its own top inset instead. -->
        <Grid
            x:Name="TitleStrip"
            Height="96"
            VerticalAlignment="Top"
            Background="{StaticResource PlayerTopScrim}"
            Padding="16,0,0,0"
            ColumnSpacing="8"
            Visibility="Collapsed">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <!-- Every glyph-only control from here down carries an AutomationProperties.Name, and they are
                 not decoration. A Button whose whole content is a FontIcon has no text for a screen reader
                 to read: the glyph is a private-use codepoint in an icon font, so Narrator announces the
                 control as 「按钮」 and nothing else — nine of the transport's twelve buttons were that. The
                 tooltip does not stand in for it (a tooltip is a pointer affordance, and this is the case
                 where there is no pointer), and neither does the name of the command behind it.

                 Where the player also owns a single key for the same action, that key is AcceleratorKey
                 rather than part of the name, which is what keeps the announcement 「返回」 instead of
                 「返回左括号 Esc 右括号」 while still telling a keyboard user the shortcut exists. -->
            <Button
                x:Name="BackButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_BackButton"
                Command="{x:Bind ViewModel.StopCommand}"
                Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}"
                VerticalAlignment="Top"
                Margin="0,10,0,0"
                AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="Esc">
                <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE72B;" Style="{StaticResource OsdGlyphStyle}" />
            </Button>

            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="4,12,0,0">
                <TextBlock
                    x:Name="TitleText"
                    FontSize="{StaticResource EgTitleFontSize}"
                    FontWeight="SemiBold"
                    MaxLines="1"
                    Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.Title, Mode=OneWay}"
                    TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                <TextBlock
                    x:Name="SubtitleText"
                    FontSize="{StaticResource EgCaptionFontSize}"
                    Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkDimBrush}"
                    MaxLines="1"
                    Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.Subtitle, Mode=OneWay}"
                    TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                    Visibility="{x:Bind ViewModel.SubtitleVisibility, Mode=OneWay}" />
            </StackPanel>

            <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Top" Spacing="2" Margin="0,10,0,0">
                <!-- 需求 7：字幕字体. A search box rather than a menu of families: there are a few thousand of
                     them on a machine with fonts installed, and no flyout that long is navigable. The rows
                     are drawn in the family they offer, same as the 设置 card's list.

                     The text is the search, not the value — taking the keyboard clears it and opens the
                     whole list, and leaving puts the family currently in use back. The current family also
                     stays in the list whatever is typed (SettingFontRow.Refilter has the why), so a search
                     that matches nothing still has one row; Resolve is what keeps Enter from picking it by
                     accident.

                     Everything the box does to the rest of the player is in PlayerPage.Fonts.cs: it pins the
                     chrome while it has focus, and it silences the player's single-letter keys — F, M, N, P
                     and Space are all commands and all letters a font name is spelled with. -->
                <AutoSuggestBox
                    x:Name="FontBox"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_FontBox"
                    Width="188"
                    Margin="0,0,6,0"
                    VerticalAlignment="Top"
                    GotFocus="OnFontBoxFocus"
                    ItemsSource="{x:Bind ViewModel.SubtitleFont.Matches, Mode=OneWay}"
                    KeyDown="OnFontBoxKeyDown"
                    LostFocus="OnFontBoxBlur"
                    QuerySubmitted="OnFontQuerySubmitted"
                    SuggestionChosen="OnFontSuggestionChosen"
                    TextChanged="OnFontBoxTextChanged"
                    TextMemberPath="Name">
                    <AutoSuggestBox.QueryIcon>
                        <SymbolIcon Symbol="Find" />
                    </AutoSuggestBox.QueryIcon>
                    <AutoSuggestBox.ItemTemplate>
                        <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:FontOption">
                            <StackPanel Padding="0,2,0,2">
                                <TextBlock
                                    FontFamily="{x:Bind Preview}"
                                    FontSize="15"
                                    Text="{x:Bind Name}"
                                    TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                                <TextBlock
                                    FontFamily="{x:Bind Preview}"
                                    FontSize="12"
                                    Opacity="0.7"
                                    Text="{x:Bind Localized}"
                                    TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                                    Visibility="{x:Bind LocalizedVisibility}" />
                            </StackPanel>
                        </DataTemplate>
                    </AutoSuggestBox.ItemTemplate>
                </AutoSuggestBox>

                <!-- 统计. A toggle, because the panel it opens stays up until it is closed again: it is
                     read while something is going wrong, and a readout that vanished when the chrome
                     hid itself would be gone exactly when it was being watched.

                     Foreground stated rather than inherited, and it is a fix rather than a flourish: a
                     ToggleButton's default foreground is the framework's TextFillColorPrimaryBrush, which
                     ThemeHost points at the *application* theme's text colour — and an application-level
                     brush is not re-resolved by this page's RequestedTheme="Dark". Under 晴昼 that is
                     #15181D, near-black, drawn over a near-black scrim. The eleven buttons that go through
                     OsdButtonStyle never had the problem; this one and the pin beside it were the two that
                     had slipped out of PlayerPalette's 「the overlay does not follow the theme」 rule. -->
                <ToggleButton
                    x:Name="StatsButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_StatsButton"
                    Background="Transparent"
                    BorderThickness="0"
                    CornerRadius="{StaticResource EgCardCornerRadius}"
                    FontFamily="{StaticResource EgUiFontFamily}"
                    Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkBrush}"
                    IsChecked="{x:Bind ViewModel.StatsOpen, Mode=TwoWay}"
                    Padding="10,6" />

                <!-- 窗口置顶. 「置顶开启后不要改变按键颜色，绘制一个置顶开启图标来替换」.

                     A plain Button rather than a ToggleButton, and that is the whole of 「不要改变按键颜色」:
                     a ToggleButton's Checked/CheckedPointerOver/CheckedPressed states swap the content
                     presenter's Background for ToggleButtonBackgroundChecked, which ThemeHost points at the
                     theme's accent — a storyboard inside the framework's own template, so the Transparent
                     written on the element only ever governed the resting state. Six resource keys could have
                     been overridden instead (the framework does exactly that for ColorPicker's own toggle),
                     but those key names travel with the Windows App SDK, and with no toggle there is nothing
                     left to come back. What is lost with it is the Toggle automation pattern, so the state is
                     said in the name instead — see PinIndicator, in Core.

                     Two drawn pins rather than one glyph swapped between two codepoints: 「绘制一个置顶开启
                     图标」, and a glyph is also the one thing about this button no report can check — whether
                     a codepoint exists in this machine's font is a question only a photograph answers.
                     The two states differ in two ways at once, tilt and fill, because at 16 pixels a
                     difference of fill alone is not a difference anyone reads: 未置顶 is a hollow pin lying
                     at 40°, 已置顶 is a solid one standing upright.

                     Geometry: both drawn inside a 16×16 box, stroke 1.2, cap radius 0.6, head radius 3.2
                     with a 2.0 hole in the hollow one; the tilted needle runs from radius 2.6 to 7.8 about
                     the head's centre and each shape is then centred on (8,8). F1 is load-bearing — the
                     needle overlaps the head's ring, and under the default even-odd rule that overlap would
                     be punched out as a hole rather than filled.

                     Width and Height must be stated: a PathIcon neither scales its geometry nor centres it
                     (the same note is on the five title-bar icons in ShellPage.xaml).

                     The name and the tooltip are not here — SetPinned writes both, because both have to say
                     which way the switch is currently thrown. -->
                <Button
                    x:Name="PinButton"
                    Click="OnTogglePin"
                    Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}"
                    Padding="10,6"
                    AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="T">
                    <Grid>
                        <PathIcon
                            x:Name="PinOffIcon"
                            Width="16"
                            Height="16"
                            Data="F1 M 9.21,3.11 A 3.2,3.2 0 0 1 9.21,9.51 A 3.2,3.2 0 0 1 9.21,3.11 Z M 9.21,4.31 A 2,2 0 0 0 9.21,8.31 A 2,2 0 0 0 9.21,4.31 Z M 8,8.69 L 4.65,12.67 A 0.6,0.6 0 0 1 3.73,11.9 L 7.08,7.92 A 0.6,0.6 0 0 1 8,8.69 Z" />
                        <PathIcon
                            x:Name="PinOnIcon"
                            Width="16"
                            Height="16"
                            Visibility="Collapsed"
                            Data="F1 M 8,2.2 A 3.2,3.2 0 0 1 8,8.6 A 3.2,3.2 0 0 1 8,2.2 Z M 8.6,5.4 L 8.6,13.2 A 0.6,0.6 0 0 1 7.4,13.2 L 7.4,5.4 A 0.6,0.6 0 0 1 8.6,5.4 Z" />
                    </Grid>
                </Button>
            </StackPanel>

            <!-- 最小化 / 最大化 / 关闭. Real client-area buttons, because playback removes WS_CAPTION so the
                 picture can reach the top edge — Windows draws no caption buttons to inherit. Flush to the
                 corner and 32 tall, which is where the hand already expects them. 最大化 goes away in
                 fullscreen: the window's edges are the monitor's there, so the button could only do
                 nothing. -->
            <StackPanel
                x:Name="WindowButtons"
                Grid.Column="3"
                Orientation="Horizontal"
                VerticalAlignment="Top">
                <Button
                    x:Name="MinimizeButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_MinimizeButton"
                    Click="OnMinimizeWindow"
                    Background="Transparent"
                    BorderThickness="0"
                    Padding="0"
                    Width="46"
                    Height="32">
                    <FontIcon
                        FontSize="10"
                        Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkBrush}"
                        Glyph="&#xE921;"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </Button>
                <Button
                    x:Name="MaximizeButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_MaximizeButton"
                    Click="OnToggleMaximizeWindow"
                    Background="Transparent"
                    BorderThickness="0"
                    Padding="0"
                    Width="46"
                    Height="32">
                    <FontIcon
                        x:Name="MaximizeGlyph"
                        FontSize="10"
                        Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkBrush}"
                        Glyph="&#xE922;"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </Button>
                <Button
                    x:Name="CloseButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_CloseButton"
                    Click="OnCloseWindow"
                    Background="Transparent"
                    BorderThickness="0"
                    Padding="0"
                    Width="46"
                    Height="32">
                    <FontIcon
                        FontSize="10"
                        Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkBrush}"
                        Glyph="&#xE8BB;"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </Button>
            </StackPanel>
        </Grid>

        <!-- ================= 播放统计面板 =================
             Drawn here rather than by mpv's stats.lua, and not by choice: the bundled libmpv-2.dll is
             built without Lua, so the script that would have drawn it was never loaded. PlaybackStats
             owns which properties are read and what the rows say; this is where they land.

             Below the title strip and outside the reveal rule — the strip comes and goes with the
             pointer, and a panel that came and went with it could not be read.

             The top inset is absent on purpose: how far down this sits is 「under the title strip」, and
             PlayerPage.PlaceOverlays works it out from the strip's own height. It used to be written here
             as 104, which was that height plus a gap, restated in a file that would not have noticed the
             strip changing. The left inset stays — that one is this panel's own taste, not a clearance. -->
        <Border
            x:Name="StatsPanel"
            HorizontalAlignment="Left"
            VerticalAlignment="Top"
            Margin="20,0,0,0"
            Padding="14,10"
            Background="{StaticResource PlayerStatsBrush}"
            BorderBrush="{StaticResource PlayerEdgeFaintBrush}"
            BorderThickness="1"
            CornerRadius="{StaticResource EgCardCornerRadius}"
            IsHitTestVisible="False"
            Visibility="{x:Bind ViewModel.StatsVisibility, Mode=OneWay}">
            <Grid x:Name="StatsRows" ColumnSpacing="16" RowSpacing="3">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- ================= 右侧：音量条 =================
             Its own overlay rather than a slider inside the transport bar. The pointer reaches the
             right edge far more often than it reaches one particular button, and the requests were
             「鼠标滚轮调整音量时要显示音量条」 and then 「加大音量条的尺寸，显示方式改为淡入淡出，鼠标指针越接近
             右边的中心显示越明显」 — none of which a control living inside the bar could answer. It no
             longer comes up with the bar: 「显示进度条的时候不需要同步显示音量条」.

             Opacity rather than Visibility, and the only overlay here that works that way. The
             ScalarTransition below is what makes it a fade — an implicit transition, so PlayerPage's
             FadeRail assigns the strength the rule decided and the compositor spends 220 ms getting
             there, which keeps 「is the rail up」 readable as a property instead of as a frame of an
             animation. Starts at zero with hit testing off, because the shell builds this page long
             before anything plays and an invisible rail must not answer clicks meant for the picture.

             「音量条的位置是歪的，还有音量条不需要边框和上方的数字」 — three separate things, and the first
             one was the framework's:

               * No Width on the slider, and this is load-bearing. WinUI's vertical Slider template lays the
                 track and the thumb out in three columns — SliderPreContentMargin, the track's own
                 SliderTrackThemeHeight, SliderPostContentMargin — and none of them is a star, so a vertical
                 slider given a wider Width does not spread the extra space: it leaves it empty on the right
                 and the track sits left of the slider's own middle. A hard Width="44" was exactly that: a thin
                 line off to one side of a pill whose number above it and mute button below it were both
                 centred, which is what 「歪」 was. HorizontalAlignment="Center" has to stay for the same
                 reason — without it the StackPanel stretches the slider back to its width and the offset comes
                 back. ProbeRailFade measures the offset now, so nobody can put a Width back unnoticed.
               * No BorderBrush/BorderThickness. PlayerEdgeBrush itself stays — the chapter preview still uses it.
               * The number above the slider went away here and 「给音量条上方加上数字」 (2026-09-04) brought it
                 back. It is not a reversal for its own sake: the ceiling moved from 100 to 130 in between, and
                 the thumb's position cannot say which side of 100 the film is on.

             「把音量条再改大一点」 (2026-09-04), and the three overrides below are how — not a Width, for the
             reason above. Widening the two content margins symmetrically is the one way to make the grab band
             wider than the template's 32 without moving the track off centre, and it is what the note here
             used to say nothing did yet: 18 + 8 + 18 = 44, the same 44 the crooked hard Width once claimed,
             this time with the track in the middle of it. The thumb's two keys really are the Horizontal ones
             — the vertical template reads SliderHorizontalThumbWidth/Height for its own thumb, which is a
             framework quirk and not a typo here.
        -->
        <Border
            x:Name="Rail"
            HorizontalAlignment="Right"
            VerticalAlignment="Center"
            Margin="0,0,22,0"
            Padding="12,20"
            Background="{StaticResource PlayerRailBrush}"
            CornerRadius="32"
            Opacity="0"
            IsHitTestVisible="False">
            <Border.OpacityTransition>
                <ScalarTransition Duration="0:0:0.22" />
            </Border.OpacityTransition>
            <!-- Named so the self-check can count what is on the rail: the figure is otherwise the one of the
                 three with nothing watching it — a TextBlock taken away from here would leave every gate green
                 and every report line unchanged, because the narration walk stops at controls and the seven
                 decorative readouts it checks are a written-down list. -->
            <StackPanel x:Name="RailStack" Spacing="10" HorizontalAlignment="Center">
                <!-- 上方的数字. Raw to the narration: a Slider already announces its own value, and 「音量 55,
                     55」 is not a better readout than 「音量 55」. Tabular figures so 9 → 10 → 100 does not
                     shuffle the pill's width about while the wheel turns. -->
                <TextBlock
                    x:Name="VolumeText"
                    Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.VolumeLabel, Mode=OneWay}"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                    FontFamily="Consolas,Cascadia Mono,Segoe UI"
                    FontSize="{StaticResource EgBodyFontSize}"
                    Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkBrush}"
                    AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw" />

                <Slider
                    x:Name="VolumeSlider"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_VolumeSlider"
                    Height="300"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                    IsThumbToolTipEnabled="False"
                    Maximum="{x:Bind ViewModel.VolumeMaximum, Mode=OneWay}"
                    Minimum="0"
                    Orientation="Vertical"
                    StepFrequency="1"
                    Value="{x:Bind ViewModel.Volume, Mode=TwoWay}">
                    <Slider.Resources>
                        <x:Double x:Key="SliderPreContentMargin">18</x:Double>
                        <x:Double x:Key="SliderPostContentMargin">18</x:Double>
                        <x:Double x:Key="SliderTrackThemeHeight">8</x:Double>
                        <x:Double x:Key="SliderHorizontalThumbWidth">22</x:Double>
                        <x:Double x:Key="SliderHorizontalThumbHeight">22</x:Double>
                    </Slider.Resources>
                </Slider>

                <Button
                    x:Name="MuteButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_MuteButton"
                    Click="OnToggleMute"
                    Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}"
                    Padding="10"
                    MinWidth="44"
                    AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="M">
                    <FontIcon x:Name="MuteGlyph" Glyph="{x:Bind ViewModel.SoundGlyph, Mode=OneWay}" Style="{StaticResource OsdGlyphStyle}" FontSize="18" />
                </Button>
            </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- ================= 跳过片头/片尾 =================

             The one button here whose name is bound rather than written: what it says depends on what is
             being offered （跳过片头, 跳过片尾, 跳过预告）, so a fixed name would be wrong half the time.
             It needs a declared name at all because its content is a panel — a button announces its own
             content only when that content is text, and 「跳过片头」 sitting one level down inside a
             StackPanel is not text as far as the announcement is concerned. Measured: with the name
             removed this was the single control the narration probe reported as 哑的.

             No bottom inset here either: it sits above the transport bar, and the bar has no declared
             height at all — two auto rows and its padding, so its height is whatever its buttons and its
             fonts come to. PlayerPage.PlaceOverlays measures it. The 148 that used to be here was a guess
             at that height, and one larger font in the transport row would have had this offer drawn
             across the seek slider. -->
        <Button
            x:Name="SkipButton"
            HorizontalAlignment="Right"
            VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
            Margin="0,0,28,0"
            Padding="16,10"
            Background="{StaticResource PlayerSkipBrush}"
            BorderBrush="{StaticResource PlayerEdgeStrongBrush}"
            BorderThickness="1"
            CornerRadius="20"
            Command="{x:Bind ViewModel.TakeSkipCommand}"
            AutomationProperties.Name="{x:Bind ViewModel.SkipCaption, Mode=OneWay}"
            Visibility="{x:Bind ViewModel.SkipVisibility, Mode=OneWay}">
            <StackPanel Spacing="6">
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                    <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE893;" Style="{StaticResource OsdGlyphStyle}" />
                    <TextBlock x:Name="SkipText" Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.SkipCaption, Mode=OneWay}" />
                    <TextBlock x:Name="SkipTip" Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.SkipTip, Mode=OneWay}" Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkFaintBrush}" FontSize="{StaticResource EgCaptionFontSize}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- The offer's own fifteen seconds, drawn rather than merely counted: a button that is
                     about to lapse should look like one. SkipPrompt.Remaining runs 1 down to 0.

                     Raw, like the other three bars that are pictures of something already said in words:
                     inside a button it would otherwise be read out as a second child with a percentage,
                     and 「跳过片头 15 秒后消失 · 87%」 is not the button's name. -->
                <ProgressBar
                    x:Name="SkipCountdown"
                    Height="2"
                    Maximum="1"
                    Minimum="0"
                    Value="{x:Bind ViewModel.SkipRemaining, Mode=OneWay}"
                    AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw"
                    Background="{StaticResource PlayerLineBrush}"
                    Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkFaintBrush}" />
            </StackPanel>
        </Button>

        <!-- ================= 底部：进度与传输控制 ================= -->
        <Grid
            x:Name="Bar"
            VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
            Background="{StaticResource PlayerBottomScrim}"
            Padding="20,26,20,12"
            RowSpacing="2"
            SizeChanged="OnBarResized"
            Visibility="Collapsed">
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <!-- 进度 -->
            <Grid ColumnSpacing="12">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <TextBlock
                    x:Name="PositionText"
                    FontFamily="Consolas,Cascadia Mono,Segoe UI"
                    Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkBrush}"
                    Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.PositionClock, Mode=OneWay}"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center" />

                <Grid Grid.Column="1" x:Name="SeekTrack" VerticalAlignment="Center" SizeChanged="OnSeekTrackResized">
                    <!-- The buffered-ahead readout, behind the slider's own track rather than beside
                         it: two bars would have the user comparing positions instead of reading one. -->
                    <ProgressBar
                        x:Name="CacheBar"
                        Height="4"
                        Maximum="1"
                        Minimum="0"
                        Value="{x:Bind ViewModel.CacheFraction, Mode=OneWay}"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center"
                        Background="{StaticResource PlayerTrackBrush}"
                        Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerTrackFillBrush}"
                        AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw"
                        IsIndeterminate="False" />

                    <!-- 章节刻度. Between the cache bar and the slider so the thumb passes over the
                         marks rather than under them, and never hit-testable: a tick is a thing to aim
                         at with the slider, not a thing to click. -->
                    <Canvas
                        x:Name="ChapterTicks"
                        Height="12"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center"
                        AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw"
                        IsHitTestVisible="False" />

                    <Slider
                        x:Name="SeekSlider"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_SeekSlider"
                        Margin="0,-1,0,0"
                        Background="Transparent"
                        Maximum="1000"
                        Minimum="0"
                        StepFrequency="1"
                        ThumbToolTipValueConverter="{StaticResource SeekClockConverter}"
                        Value="{x:Bind ViewModel.SeekValue, Mode=TwoWay}">
                        <!-- 「去掉鼠标移到进度条上时进度条出现的白色填充物」.
                             Background="Transparent" above only settles the resting state: the framework's
                             own Slider template binds HorizontalTrackRect.Fill to it, so at rest the track is
                             empty and CacheBar shows through from behind. But the template's CommonStates
                             overwrite that Fill for PointerOver and Pressed with two theme brushes, and in the
                             dark dictionary both resolve to ControlStrongFillColorDefaultBrush — #8BFFFFFF,
                             white at 54%. It is drawn 4 pixels tall across the whole track, which is exactly
                             CacheBar's own height and width, so the buffered bar disappears under it and the
                             seek bar's unplayed half turns white the moment the pointer arrives.

                             Overridden on this one control rather than on the page or in Palette.xaml, and that
                             is the whole point: the volume rail's slider and the two on the settings page want
                             that white — it is their track, and there is nothing behind them to show through.
                             A page-level or theme-level override would take their tracks away.

                             Same mechanism LibraryPage.xaml uses for ItemContainerPointerOverBackground, and it
                             fails the same silent way — a key the template cannot find leaves the white in place
                             with nothing on screen to say so. Hence ProbeSeekTrack, which pushes the visual
                             states by hand and reads the brushes back. If that probe ever reports #8BFFFFFF
                             again, move these two keys onto SeekTrack's Grid instead: lookup walks outward from
                             the element, that Grid holds only the cache bar, the ticks and this slider, and the
                             volume rail is not inside it. -->
                        <Slider.Resources>
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SliderTrackFillPointerOver" Color="Transparent" />
                            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SliderTrackFillPressed" Color="Transparent" />
                        </Slider.Resources>
                    </Slider>
                </Grid>

                <TextBlock
                    Grid.Column="2"
                    x:Name="DurationText"
                    FontFamily="Consolas,Cascadia Mono,Segoe UI"
                    Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkDimBrush}"
                    Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.DurationClock, Mode=OneWay}"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Grid>

            <!-- 控制
                 Ranked rather than evenly weighted, as the WinForms bar was: play in the middle at
                 twice the size, the two episode steps beside it, the pickers pushed right, and the
                 things nobody presses twice an hour inside ⚙. -->
            <Grid Grid.Row="1" Margin="0,4,0,0">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="2" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <Button
                        x:Name="PreviousButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_PreviousButton"
                        Command="{x:Bind ViewModel.PreviousEpisodeCommand}"
                        Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}"
                        Visibility="{x:Bind ViewModel.EpisodeControlsVisibility, Mode=OneWay}">
                        <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE892;" Style="{StaticResource OsdGlyphStyle}" />
                    </Button>
                    <Button
                        x:Name="PlayButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_PlayButton"
                        Command="{x:Bind ViewModel.TogglePauseCommand}"
                        Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}"
                        Padding="12,6"
                        AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="Space">
                        <FontIcon x:Name="PlayGlyph" Glyph="{x:Bind ViewModel.PlayPauseGlyph, Mode=OneWay}" Style="{StaticResource OsdGlyphStyle}" FontSize="20" />
                    </Button>
                    <Button
                        x:Name="NextButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_NextButton"
                        Command="{x:Bind ViewModel.NextEpisodeCommand}"
                        Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}"
                        Visibility="{x:Bind ViewModel.EpisodeControlsVisibility, Mode=OneWay}">
                        <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE893;" Style="{StaticResource OsdGlyphStyle}" />
                    </Button>
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- What is actually being decoded — 「1080p · HEVC · 8.4 GB」. The middle of the bar is
                     the one place nothing else wants, and this is the question a viewer asks of a file
                     often enough to be worth answering without opening a dialog for it. -->
                <TextBlock
                    Grid.Column="1"
                    x:Name="SourceText"
                    Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkDimBrush}"
                    FontSize="{StaticResource EgCaptionFontSize}"
                    MaxLines="1"
                    Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.SourceLabel, Mode=OneWay}"
                    TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center" />

                <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" HorizontalAlignment="Right" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="2" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <Button
                        x:Name="EpisodeButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_EpisodeButton"
                        Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}"
                        Visibility="{x:Bind ViewModel.EpisodeControlsVisibility, Mode=OneWay}">
                        <Button.Flyout>
                            <MenuFlyout x:Name="EpisodeMenu" Placement="Top" Opening="OnEpisodeMenuOpening" />
                        </Button.Flyout>
                    </Button>

                    <Button x:Name="AudioButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_AudioButton" Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}">
                        <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE8D6;" Style="{StaticResource OsdGlyphStyle}" />
                        <Button.Flyout>
                            <MenuFlyout x:Name="AudioMenu" Placement="Top" Opening="OnAudioMenuOpening" />
                        </Button.Flyout>
                    </Button>

                    <Button x:Name="SubtitleButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_SubtitleButton" Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}">
                        <FontIcon Glyph="&#xED1E;" Style="{StaticResource OsdGlyphStyle}" />
                        <Button.Flyout>
                            <MenuFlyout x:Name="SubtitleMenu" Placement="Top" Opening="OnSubtitleMenuOpening" />
                        </Button.Flyout>
                    </Button>

                    <Button x:Name="SpeedButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_SpeedButton" Content="{x:Bind ViewModel.SpeedLabel, Mode=OneWay}" Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}">
                        <Button.Flyout>
                            <MenuFlyout x:Name="SpeedMenu" Placement="Top" Opening="OnSpeedMenuOpening" />
                        </Button.Flyout>
                    </Button>

                    <Button x:Name="MoreButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_MoreButton" Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}">
                        <FontIcon Glyph="&#xE713;" Style="{StaticResource OsdGlyphStyle}" />
                        <Button.Flyout>
                            <MenuFlyout x:Name="MoreMenu" Placement="Top" Opening="OnMoreMenuOpening" />
                        </Button.Flyout>
                    </Button>

                    <Button
                        x:Name="FullscreenButton"
                x:Uid="PlayerPage_FullscreenButton"
                        Click="OnToggleFullscreen"
                        Style="{StaticResource OsdButtonStyle}"
                        AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="F">
                        <FontIcon x:Name="FullscreenGlyph" Glyph="&#xE740;" Style="{StaticResource OsdGlyphStyle}" />
                    </Button>
                </StackPanel>
            </Grid>
        </Grid>

        <!-- ================= 浮层收起时的细进度线 =================
             Two pixels at the very bottom edge, shown only while the chrome is hidden: with the bar
             gone there is nothing on screen that says how far in we are, and the answer is worth two
             pixels. Not hit-testable — reaching for it reveals the real bar, which is the thing that
             can be dragged. Inverse of Bar's visibility, so the two are never both up. -->
        <ProgressBar
            x:Name="ThinLine"
            Height="2"
            Maximum="1"
            Minimum="0"
            Value="{x:Bind ViewModel.ThinFraction, Mode=OneWay}"
            VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
            AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw"
            Background="{StaticResource PlayerLineBrush}"
            Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkSoftBrush}"
            IsHitTestVisible="False"
            Visibility="Collapsed" />

        <!-- ================= 暂停/播放提示 =================
             「暂停后显示一秒暂停图标就行（开启播放也弄个一秒的动画），别什么进度条标题音量条都持久显示在
             画面上」. Pausing used to pin the whole chrome — bar, title strip and volume rail — for as long
             as the file stayed paused, on the reasoning that a frozen frame has nothing to watch so the way
             out should stay visible. But 「the way out」 is one bit of information, and three overlays is not
             how you spell it: they cover the frame the pause was called to look at. So the pin is gone (see
             OnStatusApplied) and this is what says it instead — one glyph, in the middle, for a second.

             The one animation in this file, and the exception to the header's 「no storyboards anywhere」.
             That rule is about reveals the pointer asks for: a control you are already reaching for must be
             there the instant you arrive, and a fade only delays it. This is the opposite kind of thing —
             nobody is reaching for it, it appears and leaves on its own, and 「弄个一秒的动画」 is the request.
             A badge that simply blinked on and off for a second would read as a glitch; growing and fading
             reads as an acknowledgement.

             Not hit-testable, and starts collapsed with zero opacity so a layout pass before the first
             pause cannot flash it.

             「不要黑色的圆形边框，只要白色的三角形就可以，然后再放大一些」 took away the dark plate and its
             hairline ring; 「暂停和开始的图标太丑了弄一个白色三角形方块和两个白色的长方块就可以」 took away the
             icon font as well; and 「点击画面暂停和开始的图标要纯白色，去掉灰色」 (2026-09-05) took away the last
             thing that was not white — the same geometry stroked more heavily behind it, which came out as a
             5-pixel grey rim hugging the shape. What is left is one white shape, and the cost of that is
             recorded rather than argued with: over a nearly white frame it is not visible at all.

             「播放页面暂停和开始的图标太丑了，你换一个」 (2026-09-06) is the latest word, and this time what he
             was looking at was the shape itself: sharp-corners-plus-stroke comes out hard-edged, every corner
             rounded only six pixels and the play triangle ending in a point. PulseArt now draws the rounded
             corners into the geometry — pause is two capsule bars (corner radius = half the bar width), play a
             triangle whose base corners round 12 and whose tip rounds 22 — at the same outer sizes as before,
             so the swap changes the softness and not the weight. Fill only: with the corners in the geometry
             there is no stroke to fill, and the self-check holds that line.

             The Canvas must keep its Width and Height. A Canvas does not measure its children, so dropping
             them measures the whole badge as 0×0 — nothing on screen, and every other reading still correct.
             It is a Canvas rather than a Grid so the path draws from (0,0) and is therefore centred by
             construction: inside a Grid it would be centred on its own DesiredSize instead, which is what the
             box's own margin (PulseArt.Box) is there to leave room for. -->
        <Border
            x:Name="PulseBadge"
            HorizontalAlignment="Center"
            VerticalAlignment="Center"
            AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw"
            IsHitTestVisible="False"
            Opacity="0"
            RenderTransformOrigin="0.5,0.5"
            Visibility="Collapsed">
            <Border.RenderTransform>
                <ScaleTransform x:Name="PulseScale" ScaleX="1" ScaleY="1" />
            </Border.RenderTransform>
            <Canvas x:Name="PulseArtBox" Width="136" Height="136">
                <Path
                    x:Name="PulseShape"
                    Fill="{StaticResource PlayerPulseBrush}" />
            </Canvas>
        </Border>
        <!-- ================= 章节缩略图 =================
             Emby's own chapter still for wherever the pointer is over the seek bar. The server drew
             these when it scanned the file, so this is a cache read rather than a decode — mpv cannot
             be asked for a frame it is not currently showing without seeking to it, and seeking to
             every pixel the pointer crosses is not a preview.

             A server that extracted no chapter images has none to send, and quite a few do not: the
             extraction is off by default on some builds and skipped for some containers. So the picture
             is collapsed rather than left as an empty frame — 「预览没有画面」 was a 212×119 hole with a
             chapter name under it — and what is left is an honest chip: which chapter, and the moment
             the pointer is on.

             Each of the three lines stands on its own, because a file can be missing any of them. No
             chapter marks at all — which is a whole class of server, not an edge case — leaves the time
             by itself, and that is still worth showing: the slider's own tooltip appears only while the
             thumb is being dragged, so hovering the bar would otherwise read as nothing at all.

             Positioned in code: Left/Top alignment plus a TranslateTransform the page sets from
             SeekTrack's own position, which is the only way the popup can sit above the bar without
             this file knowing how tall the bar happens to be. -->
        <Border
            x:Name="ChapterPeek"
            HorizontalAlignment="Left"
            VerticalAlignment="Top"
            Padding="4"
            AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw"
            Background="{StaticResource PlayerPeekBrush}"
            BorderBrush="{StaticResource PlayerEdgeBrush}"
            BorderThickness="1"
            CornerRadius="8"
            IsHitTestVisible="False"
            Visibility="Collapsed">
            <Border.RenderTransform>
                <TranslateTransform x:Name="ChapterPeekOffset" />
            </Border.RenderTransform>
            <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                <Image
                    x:Name="ChapterImage"
                    Source="{x:Bind ViewModel.ChapterStill, Mode=OneWay}"
                    Width="212"
                    Height="119"
                    Stretch="UniformToFill"
                    Visibility="{x:Bind ViewModel.ChapterStillVisibility, Mode=OneWay}" />
                <TextBlock
                    x:Name="ChapterCaption"
                    FontSize="{StaticResource EgCaptionFontSize}"
                    Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkSoftBrush}"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                    MaxLines="1"
                    MaxWidth="212"
                    Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.ChapterCaption, Mode=OneWay}"
                    TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                    Visibility="{x:Bind ViewModel.ChapterCaptionVisibility, Mode=OneWay}" />

                <!-- The hovered moment. The slider's own thumb tooltip only appears while the thumb is
                     being dragged, so hovering the track had no time readout at all. -->
                <TextBlock
                    x:Name="ChapterClock"
                    FontFamily="Consolas,Cascadia Mono,Segoe UI"
                    FontSize="{StaticResource EgCaptionFontSize}"
                    Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkDimBrush}"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                    Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.ChapterClock, Mode=OneWay}" />
            </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- ================= 换集时的遮挡层 =================
             Requirement 12. The video surface and mpv's child inside it both stay alive across an
             episode change, which is what stops the window from collapsing and rebuilding — but it
             also means the last frame of the finished episode sits there while mpv tears down one
             file and opens the next at whatever size and format it turns out to be. Nothing is wrong
             with the decode; it is simply nobody's job to have painted over it. This is that job.

             Opaque, and above everything else in the grid, so it cannot lose the race the WinForms
             cover kept losing. -->
        <Grid x:Name="Cover" Background="{StaticResource PlayerCoverBrush}" Visibility="{x:Bind ViewModel.CoverVisibility, Mode=OneWay}">
            <StackPanel HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" Spacing="16">
                <!-- IsActive follows the cover rather than sitting on True. A ProgressRing animates through
                     the compositor, which does not stop because an ancestor is collapsed — and this page is
                     built when the shell starts, so a ring left active would be spinning behind a collapsed
                     grid from launch until exit, for every minute the app is not changing episodes. Bound to
                     CoverUp rather than to the Visibility it produces so there is one source for the state. -->
                <ProgressRing
                    x:Name="CoverRing"
                    IsActive="{x:Bind ViewModel.CoverUp, Mode=OneWay}"
                    Width="40"
                    Height="40"
                    AutomationProperties.AccessibilityView="Raw" />
                <TextBlock x:Name="CoverText" HorizontalAlignment="Center" Foreground="{StaticResource PlayerInkDimBrush}" Text="{x:Bind ViewModel.CoverMessage, Mode=OneWay}" />
            </StackPanel>
        </Grid>
    </local:PictureSurface>
</UserControl>
